WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:07.410
Hello, I am David Kaplan and I am not an expert.
But I hope in this series to learn as much

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.380
as possible and explain it to you the best
way I can.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.100
I want to talk about something very serious. I want to talk about the universe
as a fruitcake.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.100
You see, a great description of the expansion of the universe

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.840
is that of
an infinite fruitcake which is expanding in all directions

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:33.000
at some constant rate, where
the little pieces of fruit are galaxies and

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.460
places where you and I live. That description
is great because it matches the geometry of

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:45.600
the universe. An infinite fruitcake has no
center and so everywhere you are you see things

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.949
expanding away from you and the things that
are farther away are expanding faster. So

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:55.299
that is exactly what is happening. We know
the universe is expanding. "Know" in the sense

00:00:55.299 --> 00:01:00.889
of we have much more evidence than for most
things that you "know" in your daily life.

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:05.350
So we are very convinced of that. What we
don't really understand is why the fruitcake

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:11.320
is so uniform — why there's pieces of fruit
roughly at constant intervals, and why the

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.480
density of the fruitcake may make it taste
quite bad, but at the same time it's uniform

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.930
throughout the cake. There's an idea that
at the beginning, or very early times of the

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:26.230
universe, the universe expanded incredibly
quickly in all directions and everything got

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:31.600
flat and everything got uniform and we call
that cosmic inflation. And we wonder if that's

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.720
actually true. It's our best guess, but we
don't know. And it was one of those models

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.170
where, when it was written down, nobody really
believed we'd have any way of testing it.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:46.909
But it was a great mathematical construction
to explain the universe. Now, we actually

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:53.810
think we can test some very explicit consequences
of the model and they are basically the ripples

00:01:53.810 --> 00:02:00.720
in the fruitcake. There are ripples that are
imprinted on the cake part of the fruitcake

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.960
and we are trying to measure those and we
now have the sensitivity. Even though the

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:12.040
fruitcake is very finely grained and the ripples
are very, very subtle, we think we can see

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.980
them. So this is about to happen, and if you
see that, then you know where the fruitcake

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:17.480
came from.

